<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muslim conquest of the Maghreb</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">647–709</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Arab conquest of North Africa · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Muslim conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> was the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> and Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> between 647 and 709. Early raids were followed by the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a> in about 670, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> resistance under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusaila">Kusaila</a> and the queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dihya">Dihya</a> and the Byzantine hold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> slowed the advance. Carthage fell in 698, and by 709 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> had reached the Atlantic, preparing the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> in 711.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-the-maghreb.jpg" alt="Muslim conquest of the Maghreb" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dr. Husein Mu'nis et al. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%81%D8%AA%D9%88%D8%AD_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%BA%D8%B1%D8%A8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By 642 Arab Muslim forces under Caliph Umar had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Soon after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> led about 4,000 troops west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> and Tripolitania. Barqa fell without opposition, the Luwata Berbers agreed to pay 13,000 dinars in tribute, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> fell after about a month. In late 645 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> briefly retook Alexandria and were stopped at Nikiou in May 646, which showed that the Muslim hold on Egypt was still insecure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 647 Abd Allah ibn Sa'd, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, led some 20,000 men into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and defeated the Byzantine army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufetula">Sufetula</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, exacting a large tribute. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a> the Muslims had to begin again. Campaigns resumed in 665, and in 670 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a> founded Qayrawan. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-muhajir-dinar">Abu al-Muhajir Dinar</a> began a policy of rapprochement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> and recruited them in large numbers. Uqba returned in 682, arrested Abu al-Muhajir and the Berber leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusaila">Kusaila</a>, and led some 15,000 troops west. In 683 he reached the Atlantic but was defeated on his return, which reversed all Muslim gains.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusaila">Kusaila</a> then entered Qayrawan unopposed and ruled most of Byzacena and Numidia for several years, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuhayr-ibn-qays">Zuhayr ibn Qays</a> defeated and killed him at the Battle of Mamma. In 694 Caliph Abd al-Malik, having won the civil war, sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-ibn-al-numan">Hassan ibn al-Nu'man</a> with 40,000 troops. They took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, ending the Byzantine presence by 698. In 704 or 705 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-nusayr">Musa ibn Nusayr</a> replaced Hassan and conquered what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> over the next decade, recruiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> on a great scale. In 709 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> lost its last strongholds in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> to Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 705 the region became a province separate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, divided into five provinces: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, Tripolitania, Zab, Near Sus and Far Sus. Most of its 50,000 Arab troops were in the east, so in the west the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> manpower. In 711 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tariq-ibn-ziyad">Tariq ibn Ziyad</a> crossed the strait into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> with 12,000 men, mostly Berbers.</p><p>The loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> deprived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> of a large granary and of tax revenue. Arabization was gradual: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> did not disappear at once, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> is documented after the conquest, though its communities faded by the 14th century. Later governors imposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, taking it by money and by enslavement, which provoked the Great Berber Revolt, and many Berbers turned to the Kharijite movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muslim conquest of the Maghreb” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It lived throughout beside the Berber languages native to North Africa, and may have survived the <b>Muslim conquest of the Maghreb</b> in some isolated place: the geographer al-Bakri describes people in Sirte speaking a language that was neither Berber, Latin nor Coptic.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
